957 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186049695 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087957 #edb/ref 1050 660.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087957 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "4ee3f6cf-7f54-503a-9d82-660a1ecf09f8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088250 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186086154 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088250 #edb/ref 1069 "q-66436" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088250 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088250 #edb/ref 1071 "The equation ${x}^{2}-8x+52=0$ can be written in the form $(x+p)^{2}=q.$ Find $p+q,$ and solve the equation." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088250 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "383c50ad-8f55-5e66-be3c-643b265bd68f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088250 #edb/ref 1074 "We first move the constant to the right-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-8x+52 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-8x & =-52\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we complete the square on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-8x & =-52 \\\\ {x}^{2}-2\\cdot 4x+{4}^{2}-{4}^{2} & =-52 \\\\ (x-4)^{2}-16 & =-52 \\\\ (x-4)^{2} & =-36\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $p=-4,\\,q=-36,$ and $p+q=-40.$\n\nFinally, we solve the equation using the square root method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-4)^{2} & =-36 \\\\ x-4 & =\\pm \\sqrt{-36} \\\\ x-4 & =\\pm 6\\,\\text{i} \\\\ x & =4\\pm 6\\,\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has two solutions, $x=4+6\\,\\text{i}$ and $x=4-6\\,\\text{i}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088740 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186088741 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088740 #edb/ref 1061 455706 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088740 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "625577f3-63da-5990-aa37-6436fc70cf20" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088740 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186088886 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088741 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186088742 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088741 #edb/ref 1061 455707 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088741 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "7641a04f-8f2b-513e-af55-334dff7e186c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088741 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186087828 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088742 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186088743 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088742 #edb/ref 1061 455711 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088742 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "04a3ac7e-6653-5ae7-bf85-4e6eb8db0752" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088742 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186087829 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088743 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186088744 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088743 #edb/ref 1061 455714 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088743 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "8514fb33-b596-528a-a07d-1f67ad500b05" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088743 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186088887 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088744 #edb/ref 1061 455715 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088744 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "402941da-4dd4-589e-92f9-62b01650ad6b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088744 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186087830 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088886 #edb/ref 1064 "Suppose that we want to solve the quadratic equation\n\n$$\nx^2 -2x +2=0.\n$$\n\nThis quadratic is written in standard form with $a=1,$ $b=-2,$ and $c=2.$ However, its discriminant is negative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{b}^{2}-4ac & =(-2)^{2}-4(1)(2) \\\\  & =4-8 \\\\  & =-4 \\\\  & <0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that the quadratic equation has no real solutions. However, if we allow complex solutions, we may solve the equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{-b\\pm \\sqrt{{b}^{2}-4ac}}{2a} \\\\  & =\\frac{-(-2)\\pm \\sqrt{(-2)^{2}-4(1)(2)}}{2\\cdot 1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\pm \\sqrt{-4}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\pm 2\\text{i}}{2} \\\\  & =1\\pm \\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation $x^2 -2x +2=0$ has two solutions, $x=1+\\textrm{i}$ and $x=1-\\textrm{i}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088886 #edb/ref 1065 2827 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088886 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "275a04ea-da37-527f-9ade-d57aa888110b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088886 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088887 #edb/ref 1064 "We can also solve a quadratic equation with complex solutions using the square root method.\n\nTo demonstrate, let's find the solutions of the equation $(x-2)^2=-16.$\n\nThe left-hand side is a perfect square. Therefore, we can solve the equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-2)^{2} & =-16 \\\\ x-2 & =\\pm \\sqrt{-16} \\\\ x-2 & =\\pm 4\\text{i} \\\\ x & =2\\pm 4\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSometimes, we need to complete the square first before applying the square root method. Let's see how." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088887 #edb/ref 1065 2829 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088887 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "145efd5f-c374-5133-89cb-39314be19dcd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088887 #edb/ref 1067 "Solving a Quadratic Equation With Complex Roots by Completing the Square" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186537219 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186538037 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186537219 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186538038 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186537219 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186538048 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186537219 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045464 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186537219 #edb/ref 1045 "Solving Quadratic Equations With Complex Roots" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186537219 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186538038 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186537219 #edb/ref 1048 #edb/ref 17592186046647 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186537219 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186049695 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186537219 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "d8473e55-ba03-5c28-a07f-8b33a1ce3d26" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186537286 #edb/ref 1080 "p+q=-40,\\,x=-4\\pm 4\\,\\text{i}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186537286 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186537286 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "1c5a9dee-19f1-525b-8742-ffc708372643" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186537303 #edb/ref 1080 "p+q=-30,\\,x=5\\pm 2